00:05:40,107 --> 00:05:46,130
This is actually recent news, both in that it happened recently, but it's about modern Crocs.

77
00:05:46,671 --> 00:05:46,971
Okay.

78
00:05:47,091 --> 00:05:55,976
So this is not fossil, but it is about the trends in saltwater crocodiles in Australia and how it may have helped their numbers bounce back to where they are today.

79
00:05:56,516 --> 00:05:57,136
Okay.

80
00:05:57,485 --> 00:06:00,546
So it is also conservation focused, which is also fitting.

81
00:06:00,566 --> 00:06:01,606
That is very topical.

82
00:06:02,146 --> 00:06:11,410
This is research by Mariana Campbell et al. in Biology Letters, and the article is by Margaret Osborne in Smithsonian Mag.

83
00:06:11,730 --> 00:06:15,231
That will be linked in the blog post that will be up after the episode.

84
00:06:15,566 --> 00:06:27,571
So this is research looking at what factors might have led to saltwater crocodiles, the estuarian crocodile in Australia, to have recovered as well as their population has.

85
00:06:28,551 --> 00:06:35,914
Large carnivores in general are conservation concerns, typically, because we do not do well with large carnivores as a general rule.

86
00:06:36,327 --> 00:06:42,690
Right. We tend to find conflict with them as our species, but also we tend to wipe out a lot of the stuff they need to eat.

87
00:06:42,810 --> 00:06:49,192
Yep. So we're either competing with them or directly trying to get them out of our way.

88
00:06:49,372 --> 00:06:49,532
Right.

89
00:06:50,172 --> 00:06:55,154
This absolutely was the case with the saltwater crocodile and many crocodilians around the world.

90
00:06:55,214 --> 00:06:58,235
Thus why there are conservation efforts for so many of them.

91
00:06:58,555 --> 00:06:59,255
Hence Croc Month.

92
00:06:59,663 --> 00:07:06,167
They were over-hunted, unregulated hunting during the mid-20th century, just about drove them to extinction.

93
00:07:07,187 --> 00:07:12,230
It's estimated that during the 1970s, there were a few thousand saltwater crocodiles in Australia.

94
00:07:12,410 --> 00:07:13,831
Wow. That is not a lot.

95
00:07:13,991 --> 00:07:14,931
Not a lot at all.

96
00:07:15,782 --> 00:07:26,687
Afterward, regulations were put in place, protective laws, and nowadays there are thousands of crocodiles just in the Northern Territory of Australia.

97
00:07:26,867 --> 00:07:29,688
The estimate I found was about 100,000.

98
00:07:29,888 --> 00:07:30,328
Wow.

99
00:07:30,708 --> 00:07:34,450
This is back to the pre-exploitation levels, as they called it.

100
00:07:34,470 --> 00:07:35,130
So they bounced back.

101
00:07:35,230 --> 00:07:39,112
They have bounced back completely, at least in that area of Australia.

102
00:07:39,132 --> 00:07:40,192
Right. In terms of numbers.

103
00:07:40,284 --> 00:07:48,288
Yes, in terms of their numbers, not continent-wide, but they were wanting to look into why did they do so well.

104
00:07:48,308 --> 00:07:56,152
You know, what might have happened between then and now that led to such a recovery because this is not the case for all predators.

105
00:07:56,172 --> 00:08:01,355
There's plenty of predators that are protected after their numbers dropped and have not bounced back.

106
00:08:01,595 --> 00:08:01,795
Right.

107
00:08:02,024 --> 00:08:06,506
That are still struggling even in the face of conservation.

108
00:08:06,526 --> 00:08:08,707
Right. Like a lot of big cats, for example.

109
00:08:09,387 --> 00:08:11,008
And so they're wondering what was happening here.

110
00:08:11,268 --> 00:08:14,049
Might it give us insights to big predator conservation in general?

111
00:08:14,509 --> 00:08:19,571
One of the things that is big when it comes to predator conservation is their prey.

112
00:08:20,405 --> 00:08:28,988
And a challenge that is seen with a lot of other big carnivores is that as their numbers increase in a now depleted habitat,

113
00:08:29,409 --> 00:08:36,412
they begin competing with each other to more extreme levels because there's not as much prey or there's not as much habitat.

114
00:08:36,826 --> 00:08:47,370
And so conserving them actually puts stress on the population as their population grows and can't find enough food or territory for each individual.

115
00:08:48,330 --> 00:08:54,212
So they decided to look at, was there a shift in what they were eating? Has what they're eating changed?

116
00:08:54,292 --> 00:08:56,533
And that's what has allowed them to be so successful.

117
00:08:56,853 --> 00:09:00,935
They looked at carbon and nitrogen isotopes in the bones.

118
00:09:01,543 --> 00:09:12,147
And these are isotope elements, you know, versions of elements. And as animals, as we eat stuff, it is incorporated into our anatomy, also into our bones.

119
00:09:12,347 --> 00:09:14,068
It is the classic, you are what you eat.

120
00:09:14,368 --> 00:09:14,548
Yep.

121
00:09:14,788 --> 00:09:19,710
So a croc's bone chemistry will, with these elements, reflect their diet.

122
00:09:19,730 --> 00:09:24,012
Yes. And different foods have different levels of these isotopes.

123
00:09:24,846 --> 00:09:35,691
So they compared bones from a museum specimens 40 to 50 years old during that low population time with modern croc samples

124
00:09:36,311 --> 00:09:43,674
and found that there were much lower values of these specific isotopes in the modern crocs versus the museum specimens,

125
00:09:44,227 --> 00:09:50,930
which indicates a more terrestrial diet from their more marine aquatic estuarian diet.

126
00:09:51,310 --> 00:09:54,311
So eating food from the land more so than from the water.

127
00:09:54,411 --> 00:09:57,832
Exactly. So that would be shifting from the typical fish and turtles,

128
00:09:58,412 --> 00:10:04,775
which are very common in croc diets and seems to have been more common in the past,

129
00:10:05,245 --> 00:10:09,687
to terrestrial animals, land animals, specifically like pigs and buffalo,

130
00:10:10,628 --> 00:10:17,951
which also could have possibly been caused by the reduction in the aquatic habitats, the estuarian habitats,

131
00:10:18,631 --> 00:10:26,594
that the crocs had to live in either by size or just by yield of prey that they may not have yielded as much food.

132
00:10:27,087 --> 00:10:32,089
This gave them the idea that it was likely pigs that they were shifting to eating

133
00:10:32,369 --> 00:10:38,972
because during this time we also see an increase in the population of feral hogs in Australia.

134
00:10:39,452 --> 00:10:40,212
Something we put there.

135
00:10:40,252 --> 00:10:41,913
Yes, invasive pigs.

136
00:10:42,493 --> 00:10:46,675
Populations have been growing during that time and still to this day.

137
00:10:47,324 --> 00:10:53,567
And that is very likely a big part of that new terrestrial diet.

138
00:10:53,967 --> 00:10:58,609
Yeah, so as their aquatic food became less viable for whatever reason,

139
00:10:59,069 --> 00:11:04,432
they were able to subsist off of terrestrial food because humans had flooded the continent with pigs.

140
00:11:04,732 --> 00:11:11,915
Or as their populations increased after protection, there may not have been enough food for that new increased population.

141
00:11:12,385 --> 00:11:12,926
Oh yeah.

142
00:11:13,206 --> 00:11:20,388
And so this new land food, which there was plenty of and excess of, was there to supply them.

143
00:11:20,708 --> 00:11:28,791
One of the researchers said there's nothing else on the floodplains in enough biomass to feed the number of crocodiles other than the pigs.

144
00:11:29,011 --> 00:11:29,351
Wow.

145
00:11:29,391 --> 00:11:32,713
So it's really kind of got to be due to the pigs.

146
00:11:33,283 --> 00:11:41,087
And that this shift in diet may be why we saw such a success rate with this territory of crocodiles.

147
00:11:41,487 --> 00:11:51,251
That's fascinating because it indicates, for one thing, it implies that the ability to eat a variety of different food is part of what lets these crocs be so successful.

148
00:11:51,671 --> 00:11:53,112
Which makes perfect sense.

149
00:11:53,645 --> 00:12:00,887
That is something we see throughout time, that if you can adjust your habits as a species, you're more likely to survive disturbances.

150
00:12:01,307 --> 00:12:09,409
But also, in case anybody needed another reason not to introduce invasive large herbivores, you might get more crocs.

151
00:12:09,830 --> 00:12:12,971
Or a reason to do it, which is not advice.

152
00:12:13,811 --> 00:12:15,071
Just saying, silver lining.

153
00:12:16,072 --> 00:12:18,132
This is interesting for a few reasons.

154
00:12:18,384 --> 00:12:33,091
As you said, their flexible diet seems to have been a success strategy, but also it may give an answer as to why they have not increased in the same way in other parts of Australia, where the feral pig populations are not as notable or may not be big enough.

155
00:12:33,431 --> 00:12:40,775
Right, where you can have a flexible diet behavior-wise, but if there isn't something to eat, then that's not going to help you.

156
00:12:40,987 --> 00:12:46,889
Yep. This also, they made the note, means that crocs are helping mitigate this invasive species.

157
00:12:47,249 --> 00:12:48,369
Oh yeah, thanks crocs.

158
00:12:48,389 --> 00:12:51,070
So that's another reason to protect the crocs.

159
00:12:51,090 --> 00:12:54,531
Oh my goodness, there'd be even more feral hogs if it weren't for the crocs.

160
00:12:55,432 --> 00:13:03,414
They said that feral hogs are one of the most destructive invasive species, but also their population increase has been ridiculous.

161
00:13:04,046 --> 00:13:12,489
One of the researchers was quoted saying, basically they have no idea how big the actual population of feral hogs in Australia is, because of how quickly it's grown.

162
00:13:12,849 --> 00:13:22,653
There's just no, it's out of control, and there's no metrics on it, but the crocs seem like they might actually be doing a huge part toward helping.

163
00:13:22,727 --> 00:13:30,429
Yeah, what happens when one incredibly successful generalist species meets another incredibly successful generalist species?

164
00:13:32,370 --> 00:13:38,932
This also means that this would result in an increased flow of terrestrial nutrients to aquatic ecosystems.

165
00:13:39,172 --> 00:13:41,333
Oh yeah, it's going to change the whole ecosystem dynamic.

166
00:13:41,353 --> 00:13:44,374
Yep, they're bringing bacon into the water.

167
00:13:44,494 --> 00:13:45,834
Yep, they're bringing home the bacon.

168
00:13:45,854 --> 00:13:57,378
Yeah, they're bringing them into by eating these pigs and then pooping in the water, so it has, this has actually shifted or could lead to larger shifts in the nutrient systems of northern Australia.

169
00:13:57,928 --> 00:14:01,570
Interesting. Fascinating. What a way to kick off croc month.

170
00:14:02,110 --> 00:14:08,093
Well, speaking of large predators, my first bit of news is not about crocs, but it is about fossils.

171
00:14:08,393 --> 00:14:08,593
Alright.

172
00:14:08,784 --> 00:14:13,026
Specifically, American cheetahs and what they were eating.

173
00:14:13,406 --> 00:14:22,590
This is research in the New Mexico Museum of Natural History and Science Bulletin by John Paul Hodnett et al. Incidentally, one of the et als in here is Jim Mead.

174
00:14:23,010 --> 00:14:23,650
Oh, hey.

175
00:14:23,910 --> 00:14:24,631
Our old buddy Jim.

176
00:14:24,711 --> 00:14:25,031
Hey, Jim.

177
00:14:25,691 --> 00:14:30,433
And in the blog post, we will link to an article in Live Science by Stephanie Pappas.

178
00:14:30,964 --> 00:14:43,810
Miracinonyx is the scientific name of the big cat often called the American cheetah. We've talked about this before. This was a cat that lived here in North America that is often compared to modern day cheetahs.

179
00:14:43,890 --> 00:14:44,150
Yes.

180
00:14:44,791 --> 00:14:53,154
Often envisioned as running across the Pleistocene savannas, hunting down fast prey like pronghorns, which we still have in this continent today.

181
00:14:53,846 --> 00:15:02,810
Although, and we talked about this back in the cats episode, episode 93, recent research has suggested that American cheetahs might have been closer related to mountain lions.

182
00:15:03,070 --> 00:15:03,250
Yeah.

183
00:15:03,430 --> 00:15:08,493
Cougars, pumas, those are all the same thing, mountain lions, rather than the African cheetahs that we have today.

184
00:15:08,713 --> 00:15:12,354
Yes, but that they were, they still seem like they were shaped like a cheetah.

185
00:15:12,385 --> 00:15:21,409
And perhaps living the same way. This research finds new records of occurrence of American cheetahs that maybe not.

186
00:15:22,129 --> 00:15:22,349
Oh.

187
00:15:22,830 --> 00:15:28,532
These new fossils or new, new records. Some of these have been re-identified, not necessarily newly discovered.

188
00:15:28,692 --> 00:15:30,393
That newly understood to be.

189
00:15:30,565 --> 00:15:37,828
Yes, they come from three cave sites in the Grand Canyon of Arizona, which is not a surprise knowing that Jim worked on this.

190
00:15:39,069 --> 00:15:44,531
Two of the caves included some foot bones, which they have identified as Miracinonyx, the American cheetah.

191
00:15:44,932 --> 00:15:49,334
The other cave, Rampart Cave, which I believe is famous for sloths and possibly for sloth poop.

192
00:15:49,754 --> 00:15:50,054
Cool.

193
00:15:50,343 --> 00:15:59,986
Found two partial skeletons of the cheetahs, a sub-adult, so young adult, and a kitten, which is estimated to have been about six months old.

194
00:16:00,226 --> 00:16:00,786
Adorable.

195
00:16:00,926 --> 00:16:07,309
A cute little American cheetah kitten. Some of these fossils had been previously identified as puma.

196
00:16:07,769 --> 00:16:08,269
Okay, gotcha.

197
00:16:08,289 --> 00:16:13,271
And here they are re-identifying them. No, actually these are cheetah, American cheetah.

198
00:16:13,703 --> 00:16:26,088
What stands out about this is that this area, the Grand Canyon region, now and considering especially back in the late Pleistocene, is not an open savanna-like habitat.

199
00:16:26,508 --> 00:16:38,573
Instead, it's more dry, upland, rocky, canyon kind of environment, which is not what you would typically think of a cheetah-like cat racing across the landscape chasing down prey.

200
00:16:38,708 --> 00:16:41,189
Yeah, not a lot of room to get up to speed out there.

201
00:16:41,709 --> 00:16:54,593
The authors also point out that Natural Trap Cave in Wyoming, which is also well known for American cheetah remains, is a similar habitat, not open savanna habitat.

202
00:16:55,106 --> 00:17:07,549
On top of that, they point out that in the Grand Canyon region at this time period, there don't appear to be a lot of ungulates, herbivorous mammals, that are grassland or savanna adapted.

203
00:17:08,730 --> 00:17:16,712
So all that evidence together, these researchers are saying, well, maybe the American cheetah wasn't acting like a cheetah.

204
00:17:16,768 --> 00:17:24,091
Maybe they weren't living in the right habitat with the right prey to be chasing them down like a cheetah, at least not all the time.

