00:30:07,930 --> 00:30:08,130
Yeah.

335
00:30:08,783 --> 00:30:27,109
This likely means that it was some sort of male-to-male competition, since that's what most head-geared animals in the ruminants are using their headgear for, similar to the necking that today's drafts do, where they swing their heads like a mace at each other because they're weird, which led them to another question.

336
00:30:27,526 --> 00:30:47,752
It has often been wondered why drafts evolved their long necks today, and one idea is that it was to reach higher food, that they started reaching for higher and higher food as they were able to out-compete or avoid competition, and that pressured them to evolve longer necks to increase their speciality.

337
00:30:48,151 --> 00:30:57,174
That was proposed by Darwin, that's been one of the classic ideas, but a lot of people have kind of been like, "That seems like an extremely extreme neck to be able to do that."

338
00:30:57,627 --> 00:31:16,252
Another reason that brings the long neck hypothesis into question for eating is that some people have said that it doesn't seem like the appearance of long neck drafts seems to sync up with the appearance of the environments we see them in, that may not sync up time-wise.

339
00:31:16,872 --> 00:31:18,732
So, perhaps there was another reason.

340
00:31:19,407 --> 00:31:36,491
Another likely scenario is that there's a sexual selection aspect, that for some reason, the long-necked males, or long-necked individuals, were having more success in mating, which spurred such an extreme body structure, which happens all the time with sexual selection.

341
00:31:37,086 --> 00:31:50,930
This has been called the "necks for sex" hypothesis, and with another extremely weird headgear, with extremely weird neck drafoid, kind of led them to go, "Let's test that hypothesis at the same time."

342
00:31:51,308 --> 00:32:06,792
So they took a look at the headgear of extinct relatives and species of giraffe, and they identified 14 different kinds of headgear among the giraffids, including today's giraffe and our helmets on this species.

343
00:32:07,328 --> 00:32:25,913
Which is notable because that degree of diversity in headgear is higher than every other group of pecoran, which are the group we talked about mostly in the headgear episode, which suggests that there very likely is a high, high selection pressure that syncs up with the headgear and likely the necks.

344
00:32:26,149 --> 00:32:37,012
To do weird things with everything above the shoulders. Yes, so that we might have mating behavior to blame for today's weird long-necked giraffes, more so than the tops of trees.

345
00:32:37,548 --> 00:32:53,492
That it could be that the necking combat behavior came first, and that the new eating niche was a side benefit that helped pressure it, but that the male-to-male competition could be the primary source.

