00:33:38,829 --> 00:33:49,651
This is research by Alice Buchek et al. in the journal Molecular Biology and Evolution, and we will link in the blog post to an article in Science Alert by Jacinta Bowler.

351
00:33:50,127 --> 00:34:04,791
Termites, you might have heard about them, are famously very common all over the globe. They are famous for eating wood, they are famous for being social, they are extremely widespread common and successful insects.

352
00:34:05,429 --> 00:34:20,113
This research focused on a particular family of termites, Callotermitidae, the drywood termites, which are found across tropical and subtropical regions. They are found in North America, South America, Africa, Australia, South Asia.

353
00:34:21,029 --> 00:34:30,851
Very widespread across the Earth. Drywood termites typically live in small colonies and they are usually confined to a single wood item.

354
00:34:31,292 --> 00:34:42,154
Okay, yeah. So they'll live in like a dead branch, but that's it. They don't leave the branch, they don't go anywhere else. They don't have the ability to, as the authors put it, forage outside of their wood home.

355
00:34:42,587 --> 00:34:49,889
There are other termites that will. They'll go out, they'll feed on a bunch of different things. These typically will only live in one piece of wood.

356
00:34:50,169 --> 00:35:01,433
Yeah, so our termites kind of function more like an anthill, where we have our nest but then we go and do stuff. These are just making a home and everyone lives in that home.

357
00:35:01,706 --> 00:35:13,789
Yes. This research sought to ask two questions. One, about that interesting, somewhat unusual lifestyle, but also how they got to all the various places around the world that they got to.

358
00:35:14,249 --> 00:35:25,272
Spoilers, the answer is exactly what you hope the answer is. Here is how they did it. They looked at the phylogeny of these termites, that is their evolutionary history, using mitochondrial DNA.

359
00:35:25,708 --> 00:35:41,933
So they compared the DNA across around 120 different species of these termites, which is about a third of their total living diversity, and they included representatives of almost every living genus within this group.

360
00:35:42,428 --> 00:35:52,951
And they put it all into a phylogenetic analysis to say how are they related, when did different relatives split, what is the full evolutionary history of this group.

361
00:35:53,431 --> 00:36:02,713
And they found that the last common ancestor of the living species is estimated to have existed a little over 80 million years ago, late Cretaceous, so they've been around a while.

362
00:36:03,246 --> 00:36:20,330
This was at a time when many of the southern continents were still connected. Gondwana still existed back then, which means that some of the early splits in these termites, some of the early movements to different parts of the modern world,

363
00:36:20,810 --> 00:36:27,332
might have happened before those landmasses split and then they were carried to where they ended up today.

364
00:36:27,807 --> 00:36:43,892
But most of the splits between distantly placed relatives among these termites, so almost 40 of these splits, are estimated to have happened in the last 50 million years.

365
00:36:44,268 --> 00:36:56,792
Which means these termites were finding their way to different parts of the world after those parts of the world had separated geographically.

366
00:36:57,168 --> 00:37:11,871
Dozens of times, it seems. For example, just to name one, they looked at Madagascar. This group of termites, they identified seven independent arrivals to Madagascar.

367
00:37:12,211 --> 00:37:22,814
Just within this one family of termites. And since this happened, most of these dispersals happened over the last 50 million years, they were probably sailing across the oceans.

368
00:37:23,188 --> 00:37:35,772
Now we've talked about this before, the idea of animals will get caught on a chunk of island or a big chunk of vegetation that rafts across the ocean and they happen to survive and land somewhere else.

