00:42:41,865 --> 00:42:47,387
Absolutely. You could bring it up in a crowded room, and it's likely a bunch of people would be like, "Yeah, Pangaea."

412
00:42:47,507 --> 00:43:03,392
Yeah. Pangaea, the idea that all of our modern land masses were formerly assembled into one global continental conglomeration, which we call a super continent.

413
00:43:03,887 --> 00:43:08,149
This is a very popular common idea, and it has a bit of a history.

414
00:43:08,529 --> 00:43:23,413
The notion of Pangaea, and indeed the word Pangaea, goes back to the early 1900s, over 100 years ago, popularized, or at least attempted to be popularized, by Alfred Wegener when he was pushing his wacky ideas about continental drift.

415
00:43:23,793 --> 00:43:24,333
What a kook.

416
00:43:25,566 --> 00:43:35,910
All the way back then. This was the guy who popularized the notion of a super continent, that many continents all together as a super continent.

417
00:43:36,410 --> 00:43:45,493
Which is something I can absolutely see why it might not take off right away, because that does kind of sound simplistic, of like, what if they were all together all at once?

418
00:43:45,628 --> 00:43:52,470
Alright, that's a fun idea, and if that's making you happy to think about, then you have fun, but why would they be?

419
00:43:52,830 --> 00:44:04,654
And indeed, that was the big issue. As we discussed in episode 122 about plate tectonics, Wegener was right, but he didn't have the correct mechanism to explain how the continents move.

420
00:44:04,934 --> 00:44:07,155
That's one of the biggest tragedies of ancient science.

421
00:44:07,575 --> 00:44:10,016
Right, like ancient science, like 100 years ago.

422
00:44:10,043 --> 00:44:17,266
Yeah, but like old concepts, when people are like, I think this is how it works. Yeah, but how does it work? I don't know.

423
00:44:17,286 --> 00:44:19,847
I don't know. Alright, sorry man, not gonna work.

424
00:44:20,347 --> 00:44:32,252
So, we discussed this in the plate tectonics episode, a brief review, we now understand that continents do in fact move over time, that the surface of the earth, the crust, comes in two varieties.

425
00:44:32,567 --> 00:44:40,990
Oceanic crust, which is thinner and denser, and continental crust, which is thicker and lighter, and being thicker often protrudes above sea level,

426
00:44:41,490 --> 00:44:51,034
and the crust of the earth is split into a whole variety of tectonic plates, which act as weird conveyor belts sliding past and into and away from each other,

427
00:44:51,507 --> 00:44:58,289
which causes the surface landforms of the planet to change, and the continents to shift.

428
00:44:58,809 --> 00:45:05,591
We have a lot of evidence for this, not the least of which is the fact that we now have good enough technology that we can literally watch the continents move.

429
00:45:06,112 --> 00:45:07,592
Actually measure it, year by year.

430
00:45:07,612 --> 00:45:11,573
This is no longer a theoretical thing. No, we're watching it from space.

431
00:45:12,134 --> 00:45:16,075
And with that information, we do of course now know that Pangaea is a real thing.

432
00:45:16,924 --> 00:45:27,108
If you travel back in time, roughly 200 to 300 million years ago, you could traverse the supercontinent of Pangaea.

433
00:45:27,488 --> 00:45:30,689
Which, boy, just think of the road trips that would have been capable back then.

434
00:45:30,869 --> 00:45:34,150
Oh my goodness. Almost pole to pole. Just huge.

435
00:45:34,631 --> 00:45:39,152
Now, the concept of Pangaea and its various understandings goes back over a century,

436
00:45:39,525 --> 00:45:50,149
but it is only in the last few decades, since the 70s or 80s onwards, that we have come to realize that Pangaea is only the latest supercontinent.

437
00:45:50,549 --> 00:45:59,133
That there have actually been, that Pangaea formed from disassembled continents and then broke apart into our modern disassembled continents.

438
00:45:59,527 --> 00:46:06,229
But before all that, they were assembled in a different orientation, in a supercontinent called Rodinia.

439
00:46:06,769 --> 00:46:14,192
And that before that, there was another supercontinent, often called Columbia, and maybe even others in between.

440
00:46:15,712 --> 00:46:21,014
Thus giving rise to the notion not only of a supercontinent, but of a supercontinent cycle.

441
00:46:21,306 --> 00:46:25,028
That's such a weird and very cool concept.

442
00:46:25,388 --> 00:46:38,352
Yes, the idea that the continental landmass of the Earth has repeatedly come together and split back apart at least a few times over at least a couple of billions of years.

443
00:46:39,906 --> 00:46:48,169
So this episode is not just about Pangaea, it is about the concept of supercontinent and what has happened with them over time.

444
00:46:48,569 --> 00:46:56,832
Now, the idea of the supercontinent cycle immediately brings up the obvious question, which is how many supercontinent have there been?

445
00:46:57,452 --> 00:47:02,614
We have Pangaea, we have Rodinia, we have Columbia, these are like the three common names you will often hear.

446
00:47:03,066 --> 00:47:12,790
But the question of how many supercontinents have there been is complicated, and it comes down to two complications, and that is criteria and evidence.

