00:47:25,807 --> 00:47:29,708
They'll say, "Well, like Pangaea, when all the continents were all grouped together."

450
00:47:30,089 --> 00:47:36,672
But that's not quite true, Pangaea didn't actually include all the continental landmass on Earth.

451
00:47:37,132 --> 00:47:41,634
There were pieces of continent that weren't part of Pangaea.

452
00:47:42,006 --> 00:47:49,990
It included most of it, but even Pangaea wasn't truly pan-gea, it was most gea. Most of them.

453
00:47:51,771 --> 00:47:52,391
Decent gea.

454
00:47:53,085 --> 00:48:06,630
So there have been some scientists, when I was reading various definitions of this, some have suggested that a supercontinent includes at least 75% of continental landmass, gathered together as one.

455
00:48:07,771 --> 00:48:08,971
A vast majority.

456
00:48:09,211 --> 00:48:17,034
Most of it. Others have suggested that it has to do with longevity. How long does it last? Is there a time limit?

457
00:48:17,526 --> 00:48:22,728
How long do these continents have to be united in order to count as a supercontinent?

458
00:48:22,908 --> 00:48:31,712
If the bunch of continents just come together really quick and then go apart, is that long enough to establish a supercontinent?

459
00:48:31,992 --> 00:48:32,212
Right.

460
00:48:32,332 --> 00:48:32,733
Gotcha.

461
00:48:33,173 --> 00:48:40,836
Others have said, "Well, why do we need to bother with a strict definition? Can it just be a bunch of continents?"

462
00:48:40,945 --> 00:48:44,947
If it's a big chunk of continent, then sure, that's fine.

463
00:48:45,107 --> 00:48:47,288
Does it need strict parameters?

464
00:48:47,408 --> 00:48:53,931
Right. And this is an important question because continents come together and move apart all the time.

465
00:48:54,325 --> 00:49:05,170
Like, within the last 50 million years, India, the chunk of land that is India, has moved up and joined with the rest of Asia, forming the Himalayas in the process.

