00:59:23,905 --> 00:59:26,987
But there are you can get some of that.

590
00:59:27,487 --> 00:59:32,289
So there are old so there aren't mountain ranges necessarily.

591
00:59:32,409 --> 00:59:37,772
But the deformed crust that was formerly underneath a mountain range.

592
00:59:38,332 --> 00:59:43,674
So mountain ranges form when continents often times when continents come together and it pushes up the crust.

593
00:59:44,106 --> 00:59:51,229
This not only forms high elevation land mountains but also messes up all the rock beneath the mountains.

594
00:59:51,709 --> 00:59:54,070
They're highly deformed by this pressure.

595
00:59:54,531 --> 01:00:04,975
There are belts of deformed rock that seem like they are the same thing spread across multiple modern day land masses.

596
01:00:05,365 --> 01:00:12,887
The same way we see it for Pangaean stuff but which date too much older and don't match the Pangaean configuration.

597
01:00:14,068 --> 01:00:14,508
Nice.

598
01:00:14,788 --> 01:00:19,369
So we can find examples of like North America, South America and Africa.

599
01:00:19,890 --> 01:00:25,951
These formations that line up but not in a Pangaean way and much older than Pangaea.

600
01:00:26,306 --> 01:00:42,033
So the way my brain is picturing this and is kind of loving this concept is it's like if you took a jigsaw puzzle, put it together, glued it together and then recut it with a new puzzle mold.

601
01:00:42,493 --> 01:00:50,697
You would still have the seams of the old puzzle pieces but those would no longer be the most recent configuration.

602
01:00:51,068 --> 01:00:54,069
You have a new way for them to click together.

603
01:00:54,269 --> 01:00:56,189
That's actually a really cool way to think about it.

604
01:00:56,309 --> 01:00:57,550
I also kind of want to do that.

605
01:00:58,310 --> 01:01:02,431
You have to do it several times to get what the earth is currently.

606
01:01:02,851 --> 01:01:08,353
Also you have to devour a number of the puzzle pieces.

607
01:01:08,453 --> 01:01:10,493
Alright, who's eating the pieces this time?

608
01:01:11,684 --> 01:01:18,386
Another source of evidence for ancient continental conglomeration is paleomagnetism.

609
01:01:19,227 --> 01:01:32,252
So we talked about this again in the Plate Tectonics episode that when rock is molten, little magnetic crystals in the suspension will line up with the earth's magnetic field.

610
01:01:32,667 --> 01:01:49,652
When the rock cools, they will be locked into place and then they will hold that position as the continent that they're on moves and rotates so we can read these paleomagnetic signals and go, "Okay, when this formed, this continent was in this position."

611
01:01:49,972 --> 01:01:51,973
We can figure out where it used to be.

612
01:01:52,453 --> 01:01:55,854
Based on where those things were pointing through the earth toward the pole.

613
01:01:57,004 --> 01:02:04,587
And if you get a nice sequence of age in a set of rocks, you can track the motion of a particular landmass.

614
01:02:05,007 --> 01:02:15,432
Well, sometimes you can find two different landmasses that have the same record of motion in their paleomagnetic sequence.

615
01:02:15,987 --> 01:02:24,269
Which is a good clue that these two landmasses were joined and they were traveling as buddies back then before they split up later.

616
01:02:24,589 --> 01:02:32,832
So there's a bunch of examples of paleomagnetism tracking the same path of motion for what are today different continents.

617
01:02:33,326 --> 01:02:47,790
Which is great because it's like you'd be studying it on Australia and you'd map out what its path would have been and then you'd go somewhere else on the planet, study it there, and find out, it's like, "Are you just tracing the same path?"

618
01:02:47,810 --> 01:02:56,472
Yeah, these were different, but then between 1.2 and 1 billion years ago or something, their path was exactly the same.

619
01:02:56,652 --> 01:02:57,632
I love that so much.

620
01:02:57,652 --> 01:02:58,553
Which is very cool.

621
01:02:58,962 --> 01:03:10,328
So those are a few examples of direct evidence that allows us not only to determine that there were ancient supercontinents, but what they might have been shaped like and where they might have been positioned.

622
01:03:10,348 --> 01:03:22,814
And there are also a number of proxies, so that is indirect evidences, that basically is when there is a supercontinent, the world looks more like this.

623
01:03:23,067 --> 01:03:30,009
Yeah, so for example I mentioned that when continents come together it creates a lot of extremely metamorphosed rock.

624
01:03:30,509 --> 01:03:34,230
Extreme heat and pressure as continents collide.

625
01:03:34,764 --> 01:03:57,151
Even if you don't have the lining up and the puzzle connections, if you have a time period, just a handful of hundreds of millions of years or something, where there's an unusually high amount of those kinds of rocks all over the world, that's a good indication that you had a lot of continental collision, potentially meaning a supercontinent.

626
01:03:57,169 --> 01:04:08,992
And then there could be a lot of individual continents colliding, but it's very likely that you could have had at least a bunch, a grouping.

627
01:04:10,071 --> 01:04:27,195
At the same time, well not exactly at the same time, on the other end of a supercontinent, when they spread apart, the rifting of continents is associated with certain types of igneous activity, volcanic activity, that creates certain types of igneous rocks.

628
01:04:27,755 --> 01:04:33,457
So if you have a time period with lots and lots of those, that could also be an indication that you had a lot of rifting.

629
01:04:33,570 --> 01:04:35,670
That there had been a supercontinent.

630
01:04:35,710 --> 01:04:42,233
And it was breaking up. Another popular proxy evidence is detrital zircons.

631
01:04:43,393 --> 01:04:48,035
Zircons are little minerals. We've talked about zircons before because they're all the rage in geology these days.

632
01:04:49,207 --> 01:04:59,271
Long story short, these are a type of mineral that some geologists have noted form more commonly during times that continents are coming together.

633
01:04:59,871 --> 01:05:00,371
Oh, okay.

634
01:05:00,391 --> 01:05:07,954
Because the rocks that contain these zircons are being uplifted into mountains and then eroded and producing more of this mineral.

635
01:05:08,465 --> 01:05:18,329
So it has been noted that during the time of Pangaea, you had a lot more of these zircons dating to that time period than before or afterwards.

636
01:05:18,849 --> 01:05:19,329
Gotcha.

637
01:05:19,349 --> 01:05:29,794
This is another one that some have pointed out that there is sort of a cycle of the abundance of these minerals over time, which seems to line up with other evidence of supercontinents.

638
01:05:30,265 --> 01:05:36,428
And then there's all sorts of other side effects. A lot of the evidence of supercontinents is the impact they have on the globe.

639
01:05:37,668 --> 01:05:42,290
And as you can imagine, when a bunch of continental landmass comes together, it can change a bunch.

640
01:05:42,825 --> 01:05:57,131
It can affect sea level, of course, because not only are you changing how much sea there is, but when continents rift, new ocean crust is less dense and thus higher up than older ocean crust.

641
01:05:57,331 --> 01:06:06,374
So that can affect your sea level. It can affect climate patterns, how the wind moves across landmasses. It can affect ocean currents.

642
01:06:07,108 --> 01:06:13,309
You can also get different, back in our paleoclimate episode 113, we talked about the albedo effect.

643
01:06:14,310 --> 01:06:21,332
The distribution of your landmasses versus oceans can affect how much radiation is reflected back out to space.

644
01:06:21,892 --> 01:06:28,594
When you have lots of new mountains, that can create more erosion and more weathering, which in turn can also affect the climate.

645
01:06:28,906 --> 01:06:37,229
There are all sorts of different ways that having continents coming together and colliding and creating mountains and closing oceans,

646
01:06:37,749 --> 01:06:50,334
and then the reverse can impact the shape of the surface of the planet in chemical ways, atmospheric ways, all sorts of things that you can look for in the geologic record.

647
01:06:50,428 --> 01:06:59,371
It might not seem immediately clear why putting all the land together would really change the way the world works.

648
01:07:00,411 --> 01:07:06,313
If you just push all the continents together, you haven't created more continent. It's the same amount of land, but they're just next to each other.

649
01:07:06,413 --> 01:07:11,955
Why would that function differently? The example that comes to mind is when you're cooking stuff.

650
01:07:12,186 --> 01:07:19,409
If you take a bunch of dough and cook it in a big lump, one big piece, or make it into a bunch of biscuit pieces, they're going to cook differently.

651
01:07:20,129 --> 01:07:28,212
The amount of time you need or the amount of heat you need or the way they're going to cook is going to be very different, like baking a pot.

652
01:07:28,246 --> 01:07:40,150
If I just put a big clay lump in the kiln versus a pot, that lump's not going to be finished by the time the pot is, even if it's the same amount of clay, because physically they are different.

653
01:07:40,210 --> 01:07:46,572
These continents are interacting differently with the ocean and atmosphere around them, as well as other stuff.

654
01:07:47,106 --> 01:07:57,929
There is also evidence, apparently, that the cycling of supercontinents not only affects surface activity, but mantle activity.

655
01:07:58,130 --> 01:08:09,533
That activity in the mantle, so the layer of molten rock, sort of waxy molten, but molten rock underneath the crust, is not only part of what drives this cycle,

656
01:08:09,967 --> 01:08:21,730
mantle convection is thought to help push continental and tectonic movement along, but also that the formation of supercontinents impacts what's going on in the mantle.

657
01:08:22,231 --> 01:08:32,493
So when you have a lot of continents coming together, for example, you get a lot of subduction, that is plates being subducted underneath the continents down into the mantle,

658
01:08:32,927 --> 01:08:51,072
which creates an influx of material down into the mantle, which can change how it flows and can potentially provide the fuel, eventually, for plumes of mantle that rise up under the continent and start forming the rifts that break apart the supercontinent.

659
01:08:52,252 --> 01:08:54,893
Oh! That makes so much sense!

660
01:08:55,368 --> 01:09:04,530
So this isn't just a thing the continents that the plates are doing, it is a cycling, potentially, in mantle activity underneath the plates.

661
01:09:06,266 --> 01:09:21,751
That makes a ton of sense, because that's always been part of supercontinents that I never intuitively understood, is at what point do they start splitting? What is that that triggers that?

662
01:09:21,788 --> 01:09:34,692
Because if you eliminate the seafloor between you, where's the splitting going to come from? That does make a lot of sense for the source of this new splitting to arrive.

663
01:09:34,772 --> 01:09:46,495
And that's one potential source. You can also just get weakening of the crust for any sorts of reasons, if it's sliding past each other, or if you just had a lot of tectonic activity,

664
01:09:46,528 --> 01:09:55,610
it could create weaknesses in the crust that could lead to it thinning and letting more mantle material slowly infiltrate.

665
01:09:55,991 --> 01:10:02,672
So yeah, there's been all sorts of study into like, how do mantle dynamics affect supercontinent cycles?

666
01:10:03,033 --> 01:10:11,815
I read a bunch of it, I understood about 20% of it, so we will not be discussing it now, but feel free to look up mantle processes and supercontinents.

667
01:10:12,166 --> 01:10:17,748
You can explain the next 20% to us, and together we will get through this.

668
01:10:18,329 --> 01:10:27,352
Now, having said all this, there is plenty of evidence that can indicate both the presence of a supercontinent, even if we don't have more direct evidence,

669
01:10:27,946 --> 01:10:41,171
and direct evidence of the shape and size and duration of ancient supercontinents, but it is often very difficult to recreate exactly what a supercontinent might have looked like,

670
01:10:41,251 --> 01:10:50,674
or how long exactly it might have lasted. Pangaea is very well understood, and even then there's plenty of discussion still sort of refining things.

671
01:10:51,148 --> 01:10:56,569
The farther back we go, the harder it becomes, the less evidence survives until today.

672
01:10:56,969 --> 01:11:05,572
But, all this evidence together has given us a very good understanding of this supercontinent cycle,

673
01:11:06,047 --> 01:11:25,034
and it has led to the revelation that for at least the last couple of billion years, our planet has been in a basically constant state of either coming together towards a supercontinent, or breaking apart from a supercontinent.

674
01:11:25,489 --> 01:11:31,211
It is a state we are in today, that we are in between supercontinents on the planet today.

675
01:11:31,751 --> 01:11:39,493
Which may seem like, you know, the question I always used to ask as a kid is, what keeps bringing the continents back together and everything like that?

676
01:11:39,506 --> 01:11:51,931
You know, why aren't they just sliding around? But if you remember that we're on a sphere, if you go in a direction you're heading toward another continent, by definition, because eventually you'll come back around.

677
01:11:52,671 --> 01:12:01,974
What has been happening over the last 100 million years or so, 150, is that the Atlantic has slowly been growing and the Pacific has slowly been shrinking.

