01:17:42,072 --> 01:17:52,294
But if we were to get better and better data, they would the dates would narrow and that range would stop overlapping. So I have seen these sometimes called super cratons.

711
01:17:53,107 --> 01:18:05,351
So that is they weren't continents combined the way we think of it today. They were the much smaller, more ancient cores of our modern continents, the cratons combined together.

712
01:18:05,751 --> 01:18:13,193
I've also seen the word proto-continent brought up for some of these. There's something before continents as we think of them today.

713
01:18:13,366 --> 01:18:28,671
Continent larva. So there is some evidence in these early days for repeated assembly and breakup of bits of continent, but they may or may not be super continents the way that we think of them today.

714
01:18:29,191 --> 01:18:36,773
And a large part of that is that it's very possible that plate tectonics didn't work quite the same way back then.

715
01:18:37,366 --> 01:18:43,808
So there is obviously a limit as to when we could possibly have a super continent and the hard limit is when we had continental crust.

716
01:18:44,588 --> 01:18:58,371
If you go back all the way to the beginning, there is a period of time in the Hadean when there wasn't enough solid crust on Earth permanently solid for us to think about a continent, let alone a super continent.

717
01:18:59,067 --> 01:19:05,708
And we like early, early days of the planet, we didn't have water. We didn't have oceans.

718
01:19:07,247 --> 01:19:35,715
So there is a point where the ingredients just aren't there. And then even as continental crust starts to appear, there may have, back to a certain point, not been enough continental crust to either create something that we would call a super continent or to fuel the super continent cycle that there may not have been enough to create those mantle dynamics that drive that repeated breakup and assembly.

719
01:19:36,129 --> 01:19:48,611
There may have been a proto version, a time period where we had something that wasn't quite plate tectonics as we think of it today, where the mantle and the crust weren't quite acting like they do today.

720
01:19:49,091 --> 01:19:53,492
And that might have meant we couldn't get super continents back to a certain point.

721
01:19:53,906 --> 01:20:07,910
I've even seen it pointed out that one potential scenario is that the way that continental crust and mantle interacted back then might have meant that there were clusters of interacting continents.

722
01:20:09,306 --> 01:20:27,991
So you could have had these down here near the south pole would all be able to come together and break apart. And then these over here on the western side would come together and break apart, but that they would not interact with each other in this kind of early version of our continent dynamics as we think of them today.

723
01:20:28,568 --> 01:20:38,670
It's often very alien to think about how different the Earth was in regards to its temperature and its atmosphere. There are periods that if you time traveled back to it, you would suffocate.

724
01:20:39,587 --> 01:20:57,751
But also, Earth as an object, as a physical structure, functioned differently in its early, early years, its early millenias, than it does now. Like, even the planet has changed over time in how it functions.

725
01:20:59,228 --> 01:21:21,973
So, like I said, there are a bunch of named potential super continents during this three and a half to two and a half billion year period, some of which are disputed, some of which are maybe, some of which maybe probably did exist to some extent, but whether it was a super continent as we think of it goes back and forth.

726
01:21:21,990 --> 01:21:36,213
So there is this wonderful period of mystery. Yeah, early history of the continents. We have some, something was happening likely. Yeah, we had continents, they were coming together and breaking apart. How much was happening might be hard to say.

727
01:21:36,369 --> 01:21:49,412
Were there actually true super continents goes back and forth, because we're still learning about that. Absolutely. The super continents that we do truly understand, most of them are still very recent.

