01:49:55,270 --> 01:49:59,992
Again, we could talk about this for a very long time. Here's just a couple of examples.

868
01:50:00,052 --> 01:50:09,556
Extremely dry climates, it has been proposed by some, might have favored the development and spread of amniotes with their amniotic eggs.

869
01:50:09,987 --> 01:50:15,049
Episode 92, which can survive farther away from sources of water.

870
01:50:15,229 --> 01:50:16,409
That's kind of their whole thing.

871
01:50:16,670 --> 01:50:20,591
Also, seed plants, which do the same thing.

872
01:50:21,051 --> 01:50:28,074
During the time of Pangaea, there were much fewer swamps than earlier, probably because of the change in climate.

873
01:50:28,394 --> 01:50:33,196
This is a time where we have all this coal from the late Paleozoic and then it disappears.

874
01:50:33,986 --> 01:50:36,167
Except, apparently, in North and South China.

875
01:50:36,687 --> 01:50:39,988
The separate bits, those places could have swamps and stuff.

876
01:50:40,608 --> 01:50:42,629
Because they were little chunks of continent.

877
01:50:43,189 --> 01:50:51,933
And of course, famously, all the continents, most of the continents being united, meant that animals and plants could potentially disperse all over the world.

878
01:50:52,406 --> 01:51:04,370
The most famous example of this is the early synapsid Lystrosaurus, which took advantage of the aftermath of the Permian mass extinction to spread across the entire continent of Pangaea almost.

879
01:51:04,830 --> 01:51:08,512
So you had these, dispersal was much easier.

880
01:51:08,532 --> 01:51:14,074
There were no oceans to worry about crossing to get from one half of Earth's land mass to the other.

881
01:51:15,226 --> 01:51:20,648
That really throws the concept of invasive species just on its head.

882
01:51:20,768 --> 01:51:27,271
Now it's worth noting, there were also vast deserts and mountain ranges, which themselves can be barriers to migration.

883
01:51:27,571 --> 01:51:31,172
Yeah, it's not that it was just a stroll from one end to the other.

884
01:51:31,746 --> 01:51:39,510
But yeah, you could have, there is the potential that barriers have been brought down to a much less extreme.

885
01:51:41,111 --> 01:51:46,653
If you can survive some of these tough landscapes, you can get basically anywhere.

886
01:51:47,266 --> 01:51:57,770
Funnily enough, having most of the continents together also means there's less coastline, because you don't have those in-between coastlines, which means there was less space for coastal ecosystems, potentially.

887
01:51:58,290 --> 01:51:59,070
Less beaches.

888
01:51:59,290 --> 01:52:00,650
Less beaches, fewer beaches.

889
01:52:01,171 --> 01:52:07,873
Now those are a few different examples I'm sure our audience can think of plenty of other impacts of a global continent.

890
01:52:08,373 --> 01:52:12,014
But also, the breakup of Pangaea is associated with changes.

891
01:52:12,266 --> 01:52:16,127
One, because all that stuff is reversed, which creates the reverse effect.

892
01:52:16,828 --> 01:52:25,871
One example, I found a 2014 study that points out the ways that the breakup of Pangaea might have impacted the spread of flowering plants.

893
01:52:27,607 --> 01:52:37,672
That A) the breakup of Pangaea reduced all that dry climate area, allowing for more humid regions, which is better for flowering plants.

894
01:52:38,132 --> 01:52:44,174
Breaking up the deserts also could have removed barriers to them spreading to different places.

895
01:52:44,415 --> 01:52:44,975
Makes sense.

896
01:52:45,463 --> 01:52:54,507
But of course also, as your landmasses separate, that separates different populations and allows for speciation.

897
01:52:54,687 --> 01:52:54,827
Yeah.

898
01:52:54,967 --> 01:52:55,807
Episode 98.

899
01:52:56,208 --> 01:53:04,511
That allows things to evolve in isolation, separate from each other, which can produce more diversity and radiations of new forms of life.

900
01:53:04,745 --> 01:53:12,868
Yeah, it's not so much that a single continent is bad for life, but it would potentially lean to a more homogenous land.

901
01:53:13,289 --> 01:53:27,435
You don't have the opportunities to split species as often, because if I can just climb this mountain range and get to the other side to see my cousins whenever I want, well then our DNA is not going to start splitting.

902
01:53:27,648 --> 01:53:31,669
You put oceans in between, that's going to cause more speciation.

903
01:53:31,749 --> 01:53:38,191
There's no sharks in the mountains. Mosasaurs in the mountains, so I can climb those. I'm not swimming over there.

904
01:53:38,671 --> 01:53:46,734
And then of course another very famous effect on life of the breakup of a supercontinent is that rifting is associated with volcanic activity.

905
01:53:47,526 --> 01:54:02,632
And when you have intense rifting and lots of volcanic activity, it can create an event that sometimes some, including us on this podcast, have given the moniker of Large Igneous Provinces.

906
01:54:03,052 --> 01:54:05,332
Oh, and stereo now.

